/** @page jni_typesguide Type Conversions Between Qore and Java

    @tableofcontents

    The \c jni module uses reflection to automatically map Java classes to Qore classes.  This class mapping and Qore
    class creation happens at parse time (when @ref jniimport "importing Java APIs" into %Qore) and also at runtime
    (if a new class is encountered that has not already been mapped).

    There are two types of conversions from %Qore to Java:
    - @ref jni_qore2java_specific "Qore to Java Defined Type Conversions": when the type is known in advance
    - @ref jni_qore2java_default "Qore to Java Default Type Conversions": when the type is \c java.lang.Object

    @section jni_qore2java_specific Qore to Java Defined Type Conversions

    The following table describes type conversions for Java types when the \c jni module must convert a %Qore value to
    the declared type:

    Qore to Specific Java Type Conversions
    |!Target Java Type|!Source %Qore Type
    |\c boolean|any; conversions are made like with @ref Qore::boolean() "boolean()"
    |\c byte|any; conversions are made like with @ref Qore::int() "int()"
    |\c char|any; conversions are made like with @ref Qore::int() "int()"
    |\c short|any; conversions are made like with @ref Qore::int() "int()"
    |\c int|any; conversions are made like with @ref Qore::int() "int()"
    |\c long|any; conversions are made like with @ref Qore::int() "int()"
    |\c float|any; conversions are made like with @ref Qore::float() "float()"
    |\c double|any; conversions are made like with @ref Qore::float() "float()"
    |\c java.time.ZonedDateTime|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date")
    |\c java.time.OffsetDateTime|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date"), with its offset
    |\c java.time.OffsetTime|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date"): its time and offset
    |\c java.time.LocalDateTime|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date"): its date and time in \
        its time zone
    |\c java.time.LocalDate|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date"): its date in its time zone
    |\c java.time.LocalTime|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date"): its time in its time zone
    |\c java.time.Instant|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date"): its point in time
    |\c java.sql.Timestamp|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date"): its point in time
    |\c java.sql.Date|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date"): its date in its time zone
    |\c java.sql.Time|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date"): its time in its time zone, \
        without fractional seconds

    For other types, @ref jni_qore2java_default "default conversions" are used.

    @subsection jni_date_time_classes Java Date and Time Classes

    The values of the Java date and time classes in the table above, also listed in @ref jni_java_to_qore, are %Qore
    dates, and the members of Java classes that have these types are declared with the @ref date_type "date" type: a
    method returning one of them returns a date, and a parameter or a field of one of these classes takes a date,
    which is converted to the class, or the Java object.

    Other Java classes describing dates and times are not converted, as they do not describe a point in time:
    \c java.time.Duration and \c java.time.Period (amounts of time, typically passed from one Java call to another,
    for example a timeout), and \c java.time.Year, \c java.time.YearMonth and \c java.time.MonthDay (partial
    dates); their values are Java objects.  A \c java.util.Date that is not one of the \c java.sql classes is a
    Java object as well.

    @section jni_qore2java_default Qore to Java Default Type Conversions

    The following table describes type conversions for Java types when the \c jni module must convert a %Qore value to
    the declared type:

    Qore to Java Default Type Conversions
    |!Source %Qore Type|!Target Java Type
    |@ref bool_type "bool"|\c boolean
    |@ref int_type "int"|\c long
    |@ref float_type "float"|\c double
    |@ref string_type "string"|\c java.lang.String
    |@ref date_type "date"|\c java.lang.Object (compile time) or \c java.time.ZonedDateTime or \
        @ref org.qore.jni.QoreRelativeTime (runtime)
    |@ref number_type "number"|\c java.math.BigDecimal
    |@ref binary_type "binary"|\c byte[]
    |@ref nothing_type "NOTHING" and @ref null_type "NULL"|\c void
    |@ref list_type "list"|java Arrays of the list type; if no list type can be found, then the Array type is \
        \c java.lang.Object
    |@ref hash_type "hash"|@ref org.qore.jni.Hash (extends \c java.util.LinkedHashMap), however any type \
        implementing \c java.util.Map that has a constructor that takes no arguments and a compatible \c put() \
        method will be constructed / converted automatically to the desired type
    |@ref code_type "code" (closure or call reference)|@ref org.qore.jni.QoreClosure
    |@ref Jni::org::qore::jni::JavaArray "JavaArray"|\c java.lang.Array
    |@ref object_type "object"|to the Java class if the Qore object is an instantiation of \c java.lang.Object, \
        otherwise @ref org.qore.jni.QoreObject
    |all \c jni objects|direct conversion

    @note
    - %Qore 64-bit integers are automatically converted to 64-bit Java long values; to get a Java \c int value; use:
      @code{.py} new Integer(val) @endcode
    - see @ref jni_arrays for more information about conversions between %Qore lists and Java arrays

    @section jni_java_to_qore Java to Qore Type Conversions

    Java and Kotlin collections convert to the most specific compatible %Qore collection type. For example,
    <tt>List&lt;Integer&gt;</tt> converts to <tt>list&lt;int&gt;</tt>, and
    <tt>Map&lt;String,Integer&gt;</tt> converts to <tt>hash&lt;string,int&gt;</tt>. Inserting a value of an
    incompatible type into the resulting collection raises a runtime type error. Maps require string keys
    for conversion to %Qore hashes; other maps retain their Java object representation.

    Generated Java method signatures for %Qore <tt>hash&lt;auto&gt;</tt> parameters use
    \c java.util.Map rather than @ref org.qore.jni.Hash. This allows Java and Kotlin map implementations
    to be passed directly. See @ref jni_compatguide for options that retain Java object representations.

    Java to %Qore Type Conversions
    |!Source Java Type|!Target %Qore Type
    |\c boolean|@ref bool_type "bool"
    |\c byte|@ref int_type "int"
    |\c byte[]|@ref binary_type "binary" (see also @ref jni_compatguide)
    |\c char|@ref int_type "int"
    |\c short|@ref int_type "int"
    |\c int|@ref int_type "int"
    |\c long|@ref int_type "int"
    |\c float|@ref float_type "float"
    |\c double|@ref float_type "double"
    |\c java.lang.String|@ref string_type "string"
    |\c java.sql.Date|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date")
    |\c java.sql.Time|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date" based on \c 1970-01-01)
    |\c java.sql.Timestamp|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date")
    |\c java.time.ZonedDateTime|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date")
    |\c java.time.OffsetDateTime|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date" with its offset)
    |\c java.time.OffsetTime|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date" based on \c 1970-01-01, \
        with its offset)
    |\c java.time.LocalDateTime|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date", no timezone)
    |\c java.time.LocalDate|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date", no timezone)
    |\c java.time.LocalTime|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date" based on \c 1970-01-01, \
        no timezone)
    |\c java.time.Instant|@ref date_type "date" (@ref absolute_dates "absolute date", UTC)
    |<tt>@ref org.qore.jni.QoreRelativeTime</tt>|@ref date_type "date" (@ref relative_dates "relative date")
    |\c java.math.BigDecimal|@ref number_type "number"
    |\c java.lang.AbstractArray and arrays|@ref list_type "list"
    |\c java.util.Map|@ref hash_type "hash" (see also @ref jni_compatguide)
    |<tt>@ref org.qore.jni.QoreClosure</tt>|@ref code_type "code"
    |<tt>@ref org.qore.jni.QoreClosureMarker</tt>|@ref code_type "code"
    |all other objects|direct conversion

    "No timezone" means that the value is taken in the current time zone.  See @ref jni_date_time_classes for the
    date and time classes that are not converted.

    @note see @ref jni_arrays for more information about conversions between %Qore lists and Java arrays

    @section jni_declared_value_types Declared Types of Java Values

    A Java value of a reference type (an object, including a \c java.lang.String or a boxed value such as a
    \c java.lang.Integer, or an array) can be \c null, which is converted to no value.  Therefore the declared
    %Qore type of a return value, a field, a static field or a constant of a reference type always accepts no value,
    and only the types of Java primitives do not.  The declared type also accepts every value the conversion yields:

    Declared %Qore Types of Java Values
    |!Java Type|!Declared %Qore Type
    |\c boolean|@ref bool_type "bool"
    |\c byte, \c char, \c short, \c int, \c long|@ref int_type "int"
    |\c float, \c double|@ref float_type "float"
    |\c java.lang.String|<tt>*string</tt>
    |\c java.lang.Boolean|<tt>*bool</tt>
    |\c java.lang.Byte, \c java.lang.Short, \c java.lang.Integer, \c java.lang.Long, \c java.lang.Character|\
        <tt>*int</tt>
    |\c java.lang.Float, \c java.lang.Double|<tt>*float</tt>
    |\c java.math.BigDecimal|<tt>*number</tt>
    |the date and time classes converted to dates|<tt>*date</tt>
    |\c java.lang.Object|\c auto
    |a class that the classes above implement or extend (ex: \c java.lang.Number, \c java.lang.CharSequence, \
        \c java.lang.Comparable, \c java.time.temporal.Temporal, \c java.util.Date)|\c auto, as the value is the \
        %Qore value of its own class (ex: an \c Integer returned for a \c Number is an integer)
    |a list|<tt>*list&lt;auto&gt;</tt> or the Java object (see @ref jni_collection_types)
    |a map|<tt>*hash&lt;auto&gt;</tt> or the Java object, or \c auto (see @ref jni_collection_types)
    |\c byte[]|<tt>*binary</tt>, or a list of integers when compatibility types are in effect
    |an array of \c T|<tt>*softlist&lt;E&gt;</tt>, where \c E is the declared type of a \c T value (ex: \
        <tt>*softlist&lt;int&gt;</tt> for \c int[], <tt>*softlist&lt;*string&gt;</tt> for \c String[])
    |any other class \c C|<tt>*object&lt;C&gt;</tt>

    A \c null value is therefore no value, also for an array, which is not an empty list, and code can assign a
    value to the type it has at parse time (ex: <tt>string s = obj.getCharSequence()</tt> when the method returns a
    \c String).

    @subsection jni_declared_param_types Declared Types of Java Parameters

    Parameters follow the same rule: a parameter of a reference type takes no value, which is passed to Java as
    \c null (ex: \c *string for a \c java.lang.String parameter, <tt>*softlist&lt;int&gt;</tt> for an \c int[]
    parameter), and only a parameter of a primitive type does not.  The variable arguments of a varargs method are
    declared in two variants (see @ref jni_array_varargs): the array as a <tt>*list&lt;T&gt;</tt>, which passes no
    value as an empty array, as Java passes when there are no variable arguments, and the variable arguments given as
    separate arguments, the first of them declared with the type \c T of the array's elements, followed by \c ...;
    when \c T is not \c auto, a third variant takes a @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::JavaArray "JavaArray" for the
    array.  An abstract method declares the array as a <tt>softlist&lt;T&gt;</tt>, which a %Qore class implementing
    it declares, and has a concrete variant for the variable arguments given as separate arguments.

    A %Qore argument selects the variant Java would select for the value it is converted to.  A string, an integer
    (passed as a \c java.lang.Long), a float (\c java.lang.Double), a boolean (\c java.lang.Boolean), a number
    (\c java.math.BigDecimal), a date (\c java.time.ZonedDateTime), a binary (\c byte[]), a list
    (\c java.util.ArrayList) or a hash (@ref org.qore.jni.Hash) is passed for a parameter of any class that the
    converted value's class implements or extends, and such a parameter is preferred over a \c java.lang.Object
    parameter, as in Java: for example, a string calls <tt>m(CharSequence)</tt> instead of <tt>m(Object)</tt>, and a
    list calls <tt>m(java.io.Serializable)</tt> instead of <tt>m(Object)</tt>.  As in Java, an integer is not passed for
    a \c java.lang.Character parameter.  For overloads that all take no value, a call passing no value selects the
    variant of the most specific class (ex: <tt>m(String)</tt> over <tt>m(String[])</tt> or <tt>m(Object)</tt>).

    A %Qore integer matches every Java integer class, so an integer selects <tt>m(Integer)</tt> over
    <tt>m(Number)</tt>, while Java selects <tt>m(Number)</tt> for a \c Long.

    @section jni_collection_types Declared Types of Java Lists and Maps

    The %Qore types declared for Java methods and fields match the values that the conversions above produce, so
    that %Qore code can be type-checked when it is parsed:
    - every Java list (a class implementing \c java.util.List, such as \c ArrayList, \c LinkedList, or a list class
      of its own) is converted to a %Qore list, so a list return value or field is declared as <tt>*list&lt;auto&gt;</tt>
    - a Java map is converted to a %Qore hash only when all its keys are strings, so a map return value or field is
      declared as <tt>*hash&lt;auto&gt;</tt> only when its key type is \c String, as given by the Java generic signature (for
      example <tt>Map&lt;String, V&gt;</tt>, <tt>TreeMap&lt;String, V&gt;</tt>, or a class declared as
      <tt>StringMap extends HashMap&lt;String, Object&gt;</tt>); any other map (a raw \c Map, a key type variable,
      \c Properties, or <tt>TreeMap&lt;Integer, V&gt;</tt>) is declared as \c auto, as it is a hash or a Java
      object depending on its keys
    - a return value or field of a class that lists and maps implement or extend (such as \c java.util.Collection,
      \c java.lang.Iterable, \c java.util.Queue, or \c java.io.Serializable) is declared as \c auto, as it is a
      %Qore list or hash when the object is a list or a map
    - a list or map parameter takes a %Qore list or hash, which is converted to an object of the parameter's class
      created with its public constructor without arguments (an interface takes an \c ArrayList or an
      @ref org.qore.jni.Hash "Hash"), or the Java object itself; a class without such a constructor raises an
      exception for a %Qore value, and takes only Java objects
    - a field accepts both the %Qore value and the Java object

    For example, a Java method returning <tt>TreeMap&lt;Integer, String&gt;</tt> returns a Java object, as its keys
    are not strings, and is declared as \c auto:
    @code{.py}
    # Java: public static TreeMap<Integer, String> ids();
    auto ids = Example::ids();
    # Java: public static TreeMap<String, Integer> counts();
    hash<auto> counts = Example::counts();
    # Java: public static ArrayList<String> names();
    list<auto> names = Example::names();
    @endcode

    @subsection jni_collection_overloads Overloaded Java Methods with List and Map Parameters

    Java methods with different list or map parameter classes can have the same %Qore signature (for example
    \c f(List) and \c f(ArrayList), which both take a %Qore list).  The method called for a %Qore value is the one
    Java calls for the Java object that the value is converted to by default: a list to an \c ArrayList, a hash to an
    @ref org.qore.jni.Hash "Hash" (a \c LinkedHashMap):
    - \c f(ArrayList) rather than \c f(List), and \c f(ArrayList) rather than \c f(LinkedList)
    - \c f(Map) rather than \c f(TreeMap), as an @ref org.qore.jni.Hash "Hash" is not a \c TreeMap
    - a method that takes an array rather than a list, as before Java list parameters took %Qore lists: \c f(Object[])
      rather than \c f(ArrayList)

    A Java object is passed to the method of its class, or of its nearest superclass or interface, as in Java: a
    \c TreeMap object to \c f(TreeMap) rather than \c f(Map).

    @subsection jni_collection_overrides Qore Classes Implementing Java Methods with Lists and Maps

    A %Qore method that overrides or implements a Java method declares the %Qore types that the \c jni module
    declares for the Java method; the Java class generated for the %Qore class has the Java method's own signature, and
    the %Qore method's return value is converted to the Java method's return type:
    @code{.py}
    # Java: public interface Source { ArrayList<String> getNames(); int getCount(); }
    class MySource inherits Source {
        *list<auto> getNames() {
            return ("a", "b");
        }
        int getCount() {
            return 2;
        }
    }
    @endcode

    @section jni_arrays Java Arrays

    Arrays are mapped directly to and from Qore lists.  When converting from a Qore list to a Java array, the list is
    scanned, and if it can be mapped to a single Java type, then an array of that type is created.  Otherwise, an
    array of \c java.lang.Object is created and the %Qore values are mapped directly to Java values.

    An empty %Qore list becomes an empty \c Object[] when no Java array component type is specified, including
    lists nested in hashes or other lists.  Empty lists remain distinct from \c null.  If a Java parameter
    specifies an array type such as \c String[] or \c long[], an empty list produces an empty array of that type.

    %Qore lists are passed by value and Java arrays are objects (which, like %Qore objects, are passed with a copy of
    a reference to the object).  To get similar functionality in %Qore, you can use the
    @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::JavaArray "JavaArray" class supplied with the \c jni module, which is automatically
    converted to a Java array object like a list, but is also passed with a copy of a reference to the object in
    %Qore.

    @subsection jni_array_varargs Java Arrays as Variable Arguments

    When a Java method declares variable arguments, such arguments must be generally given as a single array value (so
    a %Qore list or @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::JavaArray "JavaArray" object).  Consider the following example:
    @code{.py}
    StringBuilder stringBuilder("");
    lang::Class stringBuilderClass = lang::Class::forName("java.lang.StringBuilder");
    Method stringBuilderAppendLong = stringBuilderClass.getMethod("append", (Long::TYPE,));
    stringBuilderAppendLong.invoke(stringBuilder, (20,));
    printf("%y\n", stringBuilder.toString());
    @endcode

    In the above example, the \c java.lang.Class.getMethod() and the \c java.lang.Method.invoke() methods both take a
    variable number of arguments after the first argument.  In the mapped Qore class, these must be given as a %Qore
    list (as in the above example) or a @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::JavaArray "JavaArray" object.

    The variable arguments can also be given as separate arguments, as in Java: they are packed into the array for
    the call.

    Because of this, the example above also works as follows:
    @code{.py}
    StringBuilder stringBuilder("");
    lang::Class stringBuilderClass = lang::Class::forName("java.lang.StringBuilder");
    Method stringBuilderAppendLong = stringBuilderClass.getMethod("append", Long::TYPE);
    stringBuilderAppendLong.invoke(stringBuilder, 20);
    printf("%y\n", stringBuilder.toString());
    @endcode

    The arguments for the variable arguments are passed as follows, as in Java:
    - separate arguments are packed into the array, and a list among several of them is an element of the array
      (ex: <tt>String::format("%s %s", "a", "b")</tt>, <tt>Arrays::asList(1, 2, 3)</tt>,
      <tt>m.invoke(NOTHING, "a", "b")</tt>)
    - a single list is the array (ex: <tt>String::format("%s %s", ("a", "b"))</tt>), as is a single binary for a
      \c byte[] array and a single Java array object (such as a
      @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::JavaArray "JavaArray") that is an instance of the array's class
    - no argument, or no value as the single argument, is an empty array

    A Java method with fixed parameters for the same number of arguments is called instead of a varargs method for
    that number of arguments, as in Java (ex: <tt>java.util.List.of()</tt> with up to 10 elements); the variant
    taking the variable arguments as separate arguments then declares as many of them as needed to be different.

    The same applies to a public Java method with fixed parameters inherited from a parent class (ex:
    \c A.m(String) called for one argument of \c B.m(String...) when \c B extends \c A), and to an abstract method
    of a parent interface (ex: \c I1.m(String) for \c I2.m(String...) when \c I2 extends \c I1).

    The variant taking the variable arguments as separate arguments takes extra arguments (\c ...), so they are
    accepted with \c %%strict-args and \c %%require-types (both in \c %%modern); only the first of them is
    checked against the type of the array's elements when the call is parsed, and the others are converted to it
    when the method is called.

    An abstract method (ex: \c java.nio.file.FileSystem.getPath(String, String...) called through a variable of
    the abstract class) declares the array as a <tt>softlist&lt;T&gt;</tt>, which a %Qore class implementing the
    method declares as well, and its variant taking the variable arguments as separate arguments is a concrete
    variant calling the Java method of the object:
    @code{.py}
    # Java: public interface Joiner { String join(String prefix, Object... values); }
    class MyJoiner inherits Joiner {
        string join(*string prefix, softlist<auto> values) {
            return prefix + Qore::join(",", values);
        }
    }
    @endcode
    A %Qore method overriding a varargs method of a Java interface (a default method) may declare the array as
    <tt>*list&lt;T&gt;</tt> or <tt>softlist&lt;T&gt;</tt>; Java calls it with the array.

    Limitations:
    - a variant of a Java interface that is not abstract (a default method, or the variant of an abstract method
      taking the variable arguments as separate arguments) cannot be called on an object of a %Qore class
      implementing the interface, which has no Java object for it, and raises \c OBJECT-INCOMPATIBLE; without
      \c %%strict-args, the %Qore class's own method taking the array is called instead, and the further separate
      arguments are ignored, as for any %Qore method; with \c %%strict-args (as in \c %%modern), a %Qore class
      whose method is called with separate arguments from %Qore also declares a variant for them, which gets them
      all (without \c %%strict-args, the variant taking the soft list ranks higher and is called instead):
      @code{.py}
    class MyJoiner inherits Joiner {
        string join(*string prefix, softlist<auto> values) {
            return prefix + Qore::join(",", values);
        }
        string join(*string prefix, auto first, ...) {
            return self.join(prefix, (first,) + argv);
        }
    }
      @endcode
    - a Java method of a parent class with fixed parameters of other %Qore types (ex: \c A.m(CharSequence) for
      \c B.m(String...)) is not preferred for a call with one argument, as Java prefers it; the variant of the class
      is called
    - only one variant takes a @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::JavaArray "JavaArray" for a method name with several
      varargs methods (ex: \c m(String...) and \c m(Integer...), also in a parent class): it is called for any
      \c JavaArray, and a \c JavaArray of another class is converted to its elements' type, which raises an
      exception
    - the elements of a \c byte... array are given as a binary or as separate integers, not as a list
    - a @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::JavaArray "JavaArray" is the array only for a method that is not abstract, or for
      an array of \c Object
*/
